{
  "step_1": "The decision reason can be applied to the decision problem. The reason focuses on the pairwise differences of outcomes between the two lotteries. Here, the non-zero outcomes are -6000 (Lottery B) and -3000 (Lottery A), with probabilities 0.1% and 0.2%, respectively. The pairwise outcome differences can be computed: (-6000) - (-3000) = -3000, and (0) - (0) = 0. The sum of all pairwise differences (considering distinct outcomes) is -3000. Since the more favorable sum would imply less loss (i.e., closer to zero or positive), a more favorable sum would result from smaller losses. However, the rule of the reason is to prefer the lottery with the more favorable (i.e., less negative) sum of outcome differences. In this case, the sum of differences favors Lottery A (since -3000 is the net difference, and it arises from B being worse than A). Thus, the reason would prefer Lottery A due to the less severe outcome difference overall.",
